working on a film or tv set can be a lot  between long hours and harsh conditions  it can be an exhausting and dangerous  place to be  and even the most carefully staged stunt  can end in disaster  this seems especially true of netflix's  spanish crime series money heist so  let's take a look at some of the times  that the cast and crew were put in real  danger  since you've decided to hang out let's  get started shooting a show like money  heist is more than blazing hot lights  freezing cold sound stages and plentiful  tripping hazards it's also stunts and  lots of them plenty of movies and tv  shows have a scene where a person  tumbles on a rope or chain and stops  just short of hitting the ground but  there's usually a lot of digital  trickery involved not so on money heist  remember that scene where the professor  is wrapped in chains and takes a long  tumble and narrowly avoids smacking into  the concrete floor well there's a lot  that goes into executing a stunt like  that and it's still not necessarily safe  to overcome the risks of the stunt  alvero morte had to be secured in such a  way that his chains couldn't tighten or  pinch him while still appearing to  unroll around him as he falls and a  specially cut guide rope had to be  attached to his belt to stop him from  getting too close to the floor the final  effect makes for a sudden stop but  thankfully not one that landed the actor  with a face full of concrete  for our first accident well don't play  with knives kids while shooting money  heist's final season there's a scene  where hovik kuch kirian playing bogota  has a confrontation with the captured  security chief caesar gandhia played by  jose manuel poga for the scene kukuryan  has a knife in one hand and has to press  its blade against poga's skin the scene  went off just fine but there was one  problem the knife which was supposed to  be a prop was actually real and so was  the blood that was coming out of poga's  shoulder this prop mix-up thankfully  only cost the actor a few stitches and  he was immediately able to return to  work but things certainly could have  been a lot worse  since it seems like i'm fully channeling  your mom now here's another rule don't  play or run by the pool  shooting on or in the water is  notoriously dangerous for film and tv  crews  long hours in pools can drain actors and  offer a false sense of security and  shooting on the open water is even worse  miles from land and largely exposed to  the elements water shoots aren't just  perilous they're hard to get help for  when something does go wrong  so of course money heist had to get in  on that action too right  for one of the crew's dramatic escapes  out into international waters the  production moved to a shooting location  off the coast of thailand it was hot out  there with some actors reporting  temperatures of 45 degrees centigrade  that's  113 degrees for our fahrenheit using  viewers  and because the crew had escaped wearing  cold weather gear  well in cold weather gear they had to  stay  combine the heat and those clothes with  the rolling seas that they were shooting  on and the actors weren't just throwing  up and trying not to fall off the boat  they were at risk of overheating things  were so bad in fact that actor miguel  herron suffered a heat stroke saying of  the experience that it was hot as hell  and his co-star esther acebo admitted  that the conditions had the cast and  crew ready to quit then and there saying  we all just wanted to die we're sure  anyone who has ever been seasick would  agree with her  since we're already hanging out by the  pool let's talk a little about that  flooded vault scene where denver actor  jaime lorente has to dive through the  set in scuba gear to recreate the fact  that the bank of spain's gold vault will  allegedly really flood in the event of a  robbery the production had to build an  entire set on location at britain's  famous pinewood l street studios and  sink it into a pool  diving is always tricky and when you're  shooting in a submerged enclosed set  like that you're effectively cave diving  one of the most dangerous things that  humans have ever decided to do for fun  weighed down by heavy equipment and  fully submerged it can be easy for  divers to become disoriented and panic  in small enclosed spaces like the set of  course shooting on a sound stage meant  that there were plenty of safeguards in  place but being trapped down under the  water like that is always dangerous and  scary  wait for action before you go  okay  but enough of this water stuff let's  talk about something even more perilous  fire during the ammunition depot  sequence the crew of money heist stuck  to their guns of doing as many effects  practically as possible and handed actor  enrique arce who played arturo a real  flamethrower described by the actor as a  little heavy very tiring and very hot  flamethrowers are serious business many  of them can produce a flame that reaches  3000 degrees  and spraying meters of raging fire  around like that come on you don't need  us to tell you how dangerous that is  here's another fiery fact that was  probably way too tightly controlled to  be truly dangerous to anyone  but it's way too cool not to include  the set for the bank of spain's gold  museum and banquet hall was so intensely  detailed that it took 50 craftsmen  180 days to construct  that's six months of work for a location  so naturally they also had to blow it up  you only see the actual set for about a  minute and a half of screen time before  it explodes in a glorious  single take fireball and frankly we  wouldn't have had it any other way  on a show like money heist everyone gets  blown up eventually pyrotechnics like  flames sparks and controlled explosions  are staples of action filmmaking and so  are the risks according to series star  ursula corbeiro she was once crouched  low against a column waiting to return  fire during a gunfight when she received  instructions that she was clear to move  unfortunately those instructions came a  little early and a pyrotechnics effect  went off next to her head the actress  was unhurt but she said i start to smell  like burnt chicken it was my hair that  was on fire in fact it wasn't my hair it  was the wig which was even worse because  that burns more  before we go let's cook up one more  s'more by the fire after making off with  the bank of spain's gold the heist crew  renders the gold bars down into small  untraceable nuggets to do this the  showrunners even committed to showing us  real details deciding to actually build  a smelter on set and melt down the bars  on camera the bronze bars that the  smelting crew melted were very real and  so was the temperature on set the  melting point of bronze is a sweltering  1675 degrees that's enough to make even  the trained professionals the show used  uncomfortable  and there you have it la casa de papel  has been a massive hit for netflix both  here and abroad  and it's easy to see why  the unique shooting conditions and  blockbuster stunts make for a show  unlike any other  and we're just scratching the surface  be sure to sound off with some of your  favorite stunts and money heist moments  in the comments
 

